using Forge.Core.Entities;
using Forge.Core.Enums;
using Forge.Data.Context;

using Serilog;

namespace Forge.Api.Data.TrainingContent;

public class LeadsTraining : TrainingContentBase
{
    public LeadsTraining(AppDbContext db, Dictionary<string, int> slugMap) : base(db, slugMap) { }

    public override IReadOnlyList<string> Capabilities =>
    [
        "CAP-O2C-LEAD",
    ];

    public override async Task SeedAsync()
    {
        // ── Overview (Article) ───────────────────────────────────────────
        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Leads Overview",
            Slug = "leads-overview",
            Summary = "What leads are, how the sales pipeline works, lead statuses, converting leads to customers, the Leads sub-pages, and how leads connect to quotes and jobs.",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Article,
            EstimatedMinutes = 5,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 1,
            AppRoutes = """["/leads"]""",
            Tags = """["leads","sales"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Leads Overview\n\nThe Leads module is your sales pipeline. It tracks potential customers from first contact through qualification and quoting to conversion or loss. Every lead represents a company (or an individual) that might become a customer — and every customer relationship starts here.\n\n### What Is a Lead?\n\nA lead is a prospective customer record. It captures the company name, contact person, email, phone, how they found you (source), and any notes about the opportunity. Every lead also gets a **lead number** (LEAD-…) so you can refer to it unambiguously. Leads move through a defined pipeline of statuses as you work them.\n\nA lead needs either a **company name or a contact name** — for an individual with no company, the contact name alone is fine.\n\n### Adding a Lead\n\nClick **New Lead** in the page header. A short two-step panel opens: first you pick what kind of opportunity it is (Quick add, Quick quote / RFQ, Repeat, Strategic account, or Prototype / R&D), then you fill in the details. The shape you pick adds a few fields tailored to that kind of deal — see *Adding a Lead: Engagement Shapes* for the full story.\n\nThe small arrow next to **New Lead** opens a menu with the high-volume ways in: **Import bulk leads** (a spreadsheet), **Pull from worker queue** (dial through a batch), and **Start a campaign**.\n\n### The Sales Pipeline\n\nLeads progress through five statuses:\n\n1. **New** — A fresh lead that hasn't been contacted yet. This is the default status when you create a lead.\n2. **Contacted** — You've reached out to the prospect. They know who you are and what you offer.\n3. **Quoting** — The prospect has expressed interest and you're preparing or have sent a quote/estimate.\n4. **Converted** — The lead became a customer. A Customer record is created automatically when you convert.\n5. **Lost** — The opportunity didn't work out. You record a reason (competitor won, budget, timing, etc.) for future analysis.\n\nLeads can move forward or backward through New, Contacted, and Quoting freely. Converted and Lost are terminal states, though a Lost lead can be reopened back to New.\n\n### Two Ways to View Your Pipeline\n\nThe Leads page offers two view modes:\n\n- **Table View** — A sortable, filterable list showing all leads with columns for company, contact, source, status, follow-up date, and created date. Each row has a checkbox so you can select several leads and assign them to an account in one step. Best for searching and bulk review.\n- **Pipeline View** — A board with a column for each status. Drag and drop cards between columns to update status. Best for visual pipeline management.\n\nSwitch between views using the buttons in the page header. Your preference is saved.\n\n### Follow-Up Dates\n\nEach lead can have a follow-up date. When the follow-up date passes, the date displays in red (overdue) as a visual reminder. Use follow-up dates to ensure no lead falls through the cracks.\n\n### Lead Sources\n\nThe Source field tracks how leads find you — referral, website, trade show, cold call, etc. Your admin sets the list of sources, so your team can add new ones anytime. Tracking sources helps you understand which marketing channels produce results.\n\n### Converting Leads to Customers\n\nWhen a lead is ready to become a customer, click the single **Convert to Customer** button in the lead panel. It's one click — it creates the customer from the lead's company name, contact, email, and phone, changes the lead status to Converted, and takes you straight to the new customer's page. There's no multi-step panel and no separate 'Convert + Create Job' button; the credit, tax, and address details are filled in afterward on the customer page.\n\nConversion does not create a Job. Jobs are produced later from the customer's quotes and Sales Orders (see the flow below). Conversion is a one-way action. Once converted, the lead shows a green confirmation badge. You cannot convert a Lost lead — reopen it first.\n\n### How Leads Connect to Quotes and Jobs\n\nThe typical flow is: Lead → Customer → Quote/Estimate → Sales Order → Job. Converting a lead creates the Customer record. From there, you can create Estimates (ballpark figures) or Quotes (binding, line-itemized) for that customer. When a quote is accepted, it converts to a Sales Order, which can generate Jobs on the board.\n\n### Marking a Lead as Lost\n\nWhen you mark a lead as Lost (either by clicking the Lost status button or dragging the card to the Lost column in pipeline view), a panel appears asking for the reason. The lost reason is required — you can't confirm without entering one. It's valuable too: it helps you identify patterns (price too high, competitor won, bad timing) and improve your win rate over time.\n\n### Reopening Lost Leads\n\nCircumstances change. A Lost lead can be reopened by clicking the 'Reopen Lead' button in the detail view. This resets the status to New so you can work the opportunity again.\n\n### The Leads Sub-Pages\n\nUnder **Leads** in the side menu you'll find six more pages for higher-volume sales work. Each has its own training module:\n\n- **Bulk Intake** — import a spreadsheet of leads with duplicate checking.\n- **Worker Queue** — pull a batch of leads and dial through them with keyboard shortcuts.\n- **Campaigns** — group a batch of marketing leads for reporting.\n- **Suppression** — the do-not-contact list and cooldown windows.\n- **Samples** — track sample parts sent to prospects before a quote.\n- **Accounts** — group several leads under one parent company.","sections":[]}"""
        });

        // ── Walkthrough ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Leads — Guided Tour",
            Slug = "leads-walkthrough",
            Summary = "A guided tour of the leads list page: the New Lead shape picker, the more-ways-to-add menu, search, filters, view modes, and selecting leads for bulk assignment.",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Walkthrough,
            EstimatedMinutes = 5,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 2,
            AppRoutes = """["/leads"]""",
            Tags = """["leads","sales","walkthrough"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"appRoute":"/leads","startButtonLabel":"Tour Leads","steps":[{"element":"[data-testid='new-lead-btn']","popover":{"title":"Add a Lead","description":"Click here to add a lead. A two-step panel opens: first pick what kind of opportunity it is (Quick add, Quick quote / RFQ, Repeat, Strategic account, or Prototype / R&D), then fill in the details. You need a company name or a contact name — everything else is optional.","side":"bottom"}},{"element":"[data-testid='new-lead-more-btn']","popover":{"title":"More Ways to Add Leads","description":"The small arrow opens a menu with the high-volume paths: import a spreadsheet of leads, pull a batch from the worker queue to dial through, or start a campaign.","side":"bottom"}},{"element":".view-toggle","popover":{"title":"Switch Views","description":"Switch between the table view and the pipeline board. The board shows a column for each stage so you can drag leads along. Your choice is remembered next time.","side":"bottom"}},{"element":".filters-bar app-input","popover":{"title":"Search Leads","description":"Search by company or contact name. Type what you're looking for and the list narrows to match. The pipeline board filters the same way.","side":"bottom"}},{"element":".filters-bar app-select","popover":{"title":"Filter by Status","description":"Show only leads in one stage: New, Contacted, Quoting, Converted, or Lost. Pick 'All Statuses' to see everything. On the pipeline board the columns already do this, so only search shows.","side":"bottom"}},{"element":"app-data-table","popover":{"title":"The Leads List","description":"Click any row to open a lead. Each row shows the company, contact, source, its stage as a colored label, the follow-up date (red when overdue), and when it was created. Tick the checkboxes on one or more rows and an 'Assign to account' bar appears above the table so you can link them to a parent company in one step. Click a heading to sort, or use the gear to pick which columns show.","side":"top"}}]}"""
        });

        // ── Field Reference (QuickRef) ───────────────────────────────────
        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Leads Field Reference",
            Slug = "leads-field-reference",
            Summary = "Complete reference for every field, button, status, validation rule, and action in the Leads module.",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.QuickRef,
            EstimatedMinutes = 4,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 3,
            AppRoutes = """["/leads"]""",
            Tags = """["leads","sales","reference"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"title":"Leads Field Reference","groups":[{"heading":"Lead Form Fields","items":[{"label":"Lead Number","value":"Assigned for you (LEAD-…) when the lead is created. If your admin allows manual numbers you can type your own; otherwise it's shown read-only."},{"label":"Company Name","value":"The prospect company. Required unless you enter a contact name instead. Becomes the customer name if you convert the lead."},{"label":"Contact Name","value":"The person you're working with. For an individual with no company, the contact name alone is enough. If you fill it in, they become the primary contact on the customer when the lead converts."},{"label":"Email","value":"Optional. Must look like a real email address. Carried over to the customer and contact when the lead converts."},{"label":"Phone","value":"Optional. The contact's phone number. Carried over to the customer and contact when the lead converts."},{"label":"Source","value":"Optional. How the lead found you — for example Referral, Website, Trade Show, or Cold Call. Your admin sets the list of choices, and there's a blank option too."},{"label":"Follow-Up Date","value":"Optional. When you should next reach out. Can't be in the past. Turns red once the date has passed, as a reminder that it's overdue."},{"label":"Account","value":"Optional. Links the lead to a parent company (a B2B account). Available when creating a lead and when editing one; a New Account button lets you add one on the spot."},{"label":"Notes","value":"Optional. Free-form notes about the lead — opportunity details, what was said, or what they need."},{"label":"Shape extras","value":"Extra fields that appear only for the opportunity shape you picked — parts and quantities, target price, needed-by date, reference job, decision maker, champion, current vendor, project type, expected timeline. All optional."}]},{"heading":"Lead Statuses","items":[{"label":"New","value":"The starting stage for a new lead. Nobody has reached out yet. Shown as a blue label."},{"label":"Contacted","value":"You've reached out and the prospect knows who you are and what you offer. Shown as a teal label."},{"label":"Quoting","value":"You're preparing or have sent a quote or estimate. Shown as a yellow label."},{"label":"Converted","value":"The lead became a customer. Shown as a green label with a check. This is set for you by converting — you can't pick it by hand."},{"label":"Lost","value":"The deal didn't work out. You record a reason, and you can reopen it back to New later. Shown as a gray label."}]},{"heading":"Table Columns","items":[{"label":"Checkbox","value":"Select one or more rows. When any are selected, an 'Assign to account' bar appears above the table."},{"label":"Company","value":"The company name (or the contact name for an individual), shown in bold. Click the heading to sort."},{"label":"Contact","value":"The contact person. Shows a dash when empty. Click the heading to sort."},{"label":"Source","value":"How the lead found you. Shows a dash when empty. Click the heading to sort."},{"label":"Status","value":"The lead's stage, shown as a colored label: New (blue), Contacted (teal), Quoting (yellow), Converted (green), Lost (gray). You can sort and filter by it."},{"label":"Follow-Up","value":"The follow-up date. Turns red when overdue, shows a dash when not set. Click the heading to sort."},{"label":"Created","value":"When the lead was created. Click the heading to sort."}]},{"heading":"Buttons and Actions","items":[{"label":"New Lead","value":"Opens the two-step panel to add a lead: pick the opportunity shape, then fill in the details. Found in the page header."},{"label":"More ways to add leads (arrow)","value":"The small arrow beside New Lead. Opens a menu: Import bulk leads, Pull from worker queue, Start a campaign."},{"label":"Table view","value":"Switches to the sortable list view. Your choice is remembered."},{"label":"Pipeline view","value":"Switches to the board with a column for each stage. Your choice is remembered."},{"label":"Assign to account","value":"Appears above the table once rows are selected. Pick an account (or make a new one) and every selected lead is linked to it. Pick '— None —' to unlink."},{"label":"Create lead / Save Changes","value":"Saves the lead. Stays disabled while the form has a problem or a save is in progress. Hover it to see what still needs attention."},{"label":"Back","value":"In the New Lead panel, returns to the shape picker so you can change the shape."},{"label":"Cancel","value":"Closes the panel without saving."},{"label":"Edit","value":"Opens the lead for editing, already filled in with its current values."},{"label":"Close","value":"Closes the lead panel."},{"label":"Status buttons","value":"Quick buttons to move the lead to New, Contacted, Quoting, or Lost from the lead panel. Hidden once the lead is Converted or Lost."},{"label":"Convert to Customer","value":"One click turns the lead into a customer and marks it Converted, then takes you to the new customer's page. There's no extra confirmation. Only available while the lead is still open."},{"label":"Conversion does not create a job","value":"There's no separate 'Convert + Create Job' choice. Converting only creates the customer; jobs come later from that customer's quotes and orders. Credit, tax, and address details are added on the customer page afterward."},{"label":"Reopen Lead","value":"Resets a Lost lead back to New. Only shows while the lead is Lost."},{"label":"Confirm Lost","value":"Marks the lead as Lost. You have to type a reason first — the button stays disabled until you do."},{"label":"Classification labels","value":"Click the Capability fit, NDA, or ITAR label on the lead panel and pick a value. It saves right away."},{"label":"Row click","value":"Opens the lead, showing all its fields, status buttons, the Convert to Customer button, documents, and its activity history."},{"label":"Pipeline card drag","value":"Drag a card between columns on the board to change its stage. Dropping it on Lost asks you for a reason."}]},{"heading":"Validation Rules","items":[{"label":"Company or Contact","value":"Enter a company name or a contact name. Leaving both blank is the only way to be stopped."},{"label":"Email","value":"Optional, but if you fill it in it must look like a real email address."},{"label":"Follow-Up Date","value":"Optional, but it can't be a date in the past."},{"label":"All other fields","value":"Optional. No special format needed."},{"label":"Save button hint","value":"When something's missing, hover the Save button to see what needs attention."},{"label":"Can't convert twice","value":"You can't convert a lead that's already Converted or Lost. Reopen a Lost lead first."}]},{"heading":"Lead Panel Sections","items":[{"label":"Header","value":"The company name in bold, the contact underneath, and buttons to edit or close."},{"label":"Engagement signals","value":"A short playbook hint for the lead's opportunity shape, plus labels for recent communications or a stale lead when the system has those signals. Hidden once Converted or Lost."},{"label":"Info grid","value":"Lead number, status, email, phone, source, follow-up date (red if overdue), and the lost reason if lost. Empty fields are hidden."},{"label":"Classification","value":"Colored labels for Capability fit, NDA, and ITAR / export control, plus a read-only Campaign label when the lead came from one. Click a label to set it."},{"label":"Notes","value":"Shows the lead's notes. Hidden when there are none."},{"label":"Status buttons","value":"A row of buttons for quick stage changes. Hidden once Converted or Lost."},{"label":"Convert","value":"A single Convert to Customer button. Hidden once Converted or Lost."},{"label":"Converted confirmation","value":"A green check with a confirmation message. Shows only after the lead is Converted."},{"label":"Reopen","value":"A Reopen Lead button. Shows only while the lead is Lost."},{"label":"Recent communications","value":"Emails and calls the system has logged against this lead."},{"label":"Documents","value":"Files attached to the lead — drawings, specs, NDAs. Drop a file in the upload zone (up to 50 MB); download or delete with the icons on each file."},{"label":"Activity history","value":"A running timeline for the lead — stage changes, edits, notes, and conversion, newest first."}]},{"heading":"Pipeline Board Details","items":[{"label":"Columns","value":"Five columns: New, Contacted, Quoting, Converted, Lost. The Converted and Lost columns are dimmed."},{"label":"Column header","value":"The stage name and a count of how many leads are in it."},{"label":"Card content","value":"The company name in bold, the contact with their initials, the source, and the follow-up date (red if overdue)."},{"label":"Empty column","value":"Shows a 'No leads' message."},{"label":"Drag-and-drop","value":"Drag cards between any columns to change a lead's stage. Dropping on Lost asks you for a reason."}]},{"heading":"Leads Sub-Pages (side menu)","items":[{"label":"Bulk Intake","value":"Import a spreadsheet of leads. Rows are checked for duplicates and opt-outs before anything is added."},{"label":"Worker Queue","value":"Pull a batch of leads and record the outcome of each call with one keystroke."},{"label":"Campaigns","value":"Name a batch of marketing leads so you can report on it as a group."},{"label":"Suppression","value":"The do-not-contact list. Lift an opt-out per channel or clear a cooldown."},{"label":"Samples","value":"Every sample part sent to a prospect, with its status from Requested through Delivered to an outcome."},{"label":"Accounts","value":"Parent companies that group several leads. Create, edit, or delete accounts here."}]}]}"""
        });

        // ── Knowledge Check (Quiz) ───────────────────────────────────────
        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Leads Knowledge Check",
            Slug = "leads-quiz",
            Summary = "Test your knowledge of the Leads module: statuses, conversion, pipeline management, bulk intake, the worker queue, campaigns, accounts, samples, and suppression.",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Quiz,
            EstimatedMinutes = 6,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 4,
            AppRoutes = """["/leads"]""",
            Tags = """["leads","sales","quiz"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"passingScore":80,"questionsPerQuiz":10,"shuffleOptions":true,"showExplanationsAfterSubmit":true,"questions":[{"id":"ld1","text":"A potential customer called asking about your machining services. What is the first step to track this opportunity?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Create a Customer record in the Customers module"},{"id":"b","text":"Navigate to Leads, click New Lead, pick the opportunity shape, and enter the company name, contact info, and source","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Create a Quote in the Quotes module with their company name"},{"id":"d","text":"Add a Job to the Kanban Board tagged as a prospect"}],"explanation":"New sales opportunities start as Leads. Click New Lead, pick the shape that fits (Quick add is fastest), then fill in the company name, contact name, email, phone, and source. The lead starts in New status. Don't create a Customer record until the lead is qualified and ready for conversion."},{"id":"ld2","text":"You've sent an email introduction to a new lead. What status should the lead be in now?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"New — the lead hasn't responded yet"},{"id":"b","text":"Contacted — you've reached out to the prospect","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Quoting — any communication counts as quoting"},{"id":"d","text":"Converted — the lead knows about your company now"}],"explanation":"Contacted means you've reached out to the prospect. They are aware of your company. Move to Quoting only when you're actually preparing or have sent a price quote or estimate."},{"id":"ld3","text":"What is the difference between the Quoting and Converted lead statuses?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Quoting means a quote was sent; Converted means the quote was accepted"},{"id":"b","text":"Quoting means you're preparing or have sent a quote; Converted means the lead has been turned into a Customer record","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"They are the same — Converted is just the final stage of Quoting"},{"id":"d","text":"Quoting is for estimates only; Converted is for formal quotes"}],"explanation":"Quoting indicates a quote or estimate is in progress. Converted is a terminal status that means the lead has been turned into a Customer record via the Convert action. These are distinct pipeline stages."},{"id":"ld4","text":"A qualified lead is ready to become a customer. How do you convert it?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Open the lead and click the single Convert to Customer button — one click creates the customer and marks the lead Converted","isCorrect":true},{"id":"b","text":"Step through a multi-step convert panel, entering credit, tax, and address details before it saves"},{"id":"c","text":"Choose between two buttons — Convert to Customer, or Convert to Customer + Create Job"},{"id":"d","text":"Create the customer record manually first, then link the lead to it"}],"explanation":"Converting is one click. The Convert to Customer button in the lead panel creates the customer from the lead's details and marks the lead Converted, then takes you to the new customer's page. It doesn't create a job and there's no multi-step panel — credit, tax, and address details are filled in afterward on the customer page."},{"id":"ld5","text":"A lead's follow-up date is showing in red in the table. What does this mean?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The lead is marked as high priority"},{"id":"b","text":"The follow-up date has passed — it's overdue","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"The lead is about to be automatically archived"},{"id":"d","text":"Someone else is currently working on this lead"}],"explanation":"A red follow-up date means the date has passed (overdue). This is a visual reminder that you need to follow up with the prospect. The lead won't be automatically changed — it's just a visual indicator."},{"id":"ld6","text":"You lost a deal to a competitor. How do you record this?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Delete the lead from the system"},{"id":"b","text":"Change the status to Lost — a panel appears where you can enter the reason (e.g., 'Competitor won on price')","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Archive the lead by clicking the archive button"},{"id":"d","text":"Move the lead to the Converted column and add a note saying it was lost"}],"explanation":"Click the Lost status button or drag the card to the Lost column. A panel appears asking for the lost reason, which is required — you can't confirm without entering one. The reason is stored for analysis. Lost leads can be reopened later if circumstances change."},{"id":"ld7","text":"A lead you marked as Lost six months ago has come back with renewed interest. What do you do?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Create a new lead — lost leads cannot be reused"},{"id":"b","text":"Open the lead detail, click the Reopen Lead button to reset it to New status","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Change the status directly to Contacted by editing the lead"},{"id":"d","text":"Convert the lost lead directly to a customer"}],"explanation":"Lost leads can be reopened. Open the lead and click the 'Reopen Lead' button. This resets the status to New so you can work the opportunity again. You cannot convert a Lost lead directly — you must reopen it first."},{"id":"ld8","text":"In Pipeline View, you drag a lead card from the Contacted column to the Lost column. What happens?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The status changes to Lost immediately with no further action"},{"id":"b","text":"The card moves right away, and a panel appears asking for the lost reason","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"The drag is rejected — you can only mark leads as Lost from the detail panel"},{"id":"d","text":"The lead is permanently deleted from the system"}],"explanation":"Dragging a card to the Lost column brings up the lost reason panel. The card moves right away, and you must enter a reason for losing the deal. The reason is required — the Confirm button stays disabled until you fill it in."},{"id":"ld9","text":"Where do the choices in the Source menu come from?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"They are hardcoded in the application and cannot be changed"},{"id":"b","text":"They come from the admin settings and can be configured by admins","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Each user creates their own source list in their profile settings"},{"id":"d","text":"They are imported from your email client automatically"}],"explanation":"Lead sources are managed in the admin settings. Admins can add, rename, or remove sources anytime. This keeps tracking consistent across the team."},{"id":"ld10","text":"When you convert a lead that has a Contact Name of 'Jane Smith', what happens to that contact information?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Only the company name is used — contact details must be re-entered on the Customer record"},{"id":"b","text":"A Contact record is created on the new Customer for Jane Smith, marked as primary, with the lead's email and phone","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"The contact name is stored in the Customer's notes field"},{"id":"d","text":"A separate user account is created for Jane Smith"}],"explanation":"When you convert a lead that has a contact name, a contact is created on the new customer for you. The name is split into first and last name, the contact carries over the lead's email and phone, and it's marked as the primary contact — so you don't have to re-enter any of it."},{"id":"ld11","text":"You want to see only leads that are in the Quoting stage. What is the fastest way?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Switch to Pipeline View — the Quoting column shows exactly those leads","isCorrect":true},{"id":"b","text":"Export all leads to a spreadsheet and filter there"},{"id":"c","text":"Use the search box to type 'Quoting'"},{"id":"d","text":"Go to Reports and run a leads-by-status report"}],"explanation":"The Pipeline View organizes leads by status columns, so the Quoting column shows exactly what you need at a glance. Alternatively, in Table View you can use the Status filter menu to select Quoting. Both work, but Pipeline View gives you the visual overview."},{"id":"ld12","text":"What must be filled in before you can save a new lead?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Company Name, Contact Name, and Email are all required"},{"id":"b","text":"A company name or a contact name — one of the two — and everything else is optional","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Company Name and Source are required"},{"id":"d","text":"All fields are optional — you can save a completely blank lead"}],"explanation":"A lead needs a company name or a contact name (an individual with no company is fine). Email, phone, source, follow-up date, account, notes, and all the shape-specific extras are optional. If you fill in an email, it must look like a real email address. When something's missing, hover the Create lead button to see what needs attention."},{"id":"ld13","text":"A prospect emails you a parts list, quantities, and the date they need them by. Which opportunity shape fits best when you add the lead?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Quick add"},{"id":"b","text":"Quick quote / RFQ","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Strategic account"},{"id":"d","text":"Repeat / standing relationship"}],"explanation":"Quick quote / RFQ is for an inbound request with a formed need. It adds fields for the parts and quantities, a target price, and a needed-by date so that context travels with the lead. Quick add skips the extras; Strategic is for long, multi-stakeholder cycles; Repeat is for a customer-shaped lead referencing a prior job."},{"id":"ld14","text":"You have a spreadsheet of 300 trade-show contacts. What is the right way to get them into Forge?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Add them one at a time with New Lead"},{"id":"b","text":"Go to Leads → Bulk Intake, pick the strategy, upload or paste the rows, run the preview, then import the valid rows","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Create them as Customers first, then convert each one back to a lead"},{"id":"d","text":"Email the spreadsheet to your admin — only admins can add leads"}],"explanation":"Bulk Intake is built for this. Pick the strategy (for example Trade Show Follow-up), upload the CSV or paste rows, run the preview to see which rows will be created or skipped, then click Import. Duplicates, opt-outs, and rows in a cooldown are skipped for you."},{"id":"ld15","text":"In the Bulk Intake preview, a row is labeled 'Duplicate lead'. What happens when you import?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It is imported anyway and you clean up later"},{"id":"b","text":"It is skipped — the email or phone already matches a lead you have","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"The existing lead is deleted and replaced"},{"id":"d","text":"The import stops until you remove the row"}],"explanation":"Only rows labeled 'Will create' are imported. A 'Duplicate lead' row matches an existing lead by email or phone, so it is skipped rather than creating a second copy. The other rows still go in."},{"id":"ld16","text":"You're working the Worker Queue and reach a live person who is interested. Which key records that?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"E — Engaged","isCorrect":true},{"id":"b","text":"N — No answer"},{"id":"c","text":"B — Bad data"},{"id":"d","text":"S — Suppress"}],"explanation":"E records Engaged. The lead leaves the queue and moves to Contacted in the normal pipeline so a rep can take it from there. N and V put the lead back for another try, B marks it Lost (wrong number or bounce), and S records that the prospect asked not to be contacted."},{"id":"ld17","text":"What does a campaign do for a batch of imported leads?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It sends the marketing emails automatically"},{"id":"b","text":"It groups the batch under one name so you can report on it as a cohort and set a default cooldown","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"It converts every lead in the batch to a customer"},{"id":"d","text":"It assigns the leads to a sales rep"}],"explanation":"A campaign is a named cohort. Tag an import with it and every lead rolls up to that campaign for reporting; the campaign also carries the strategy and a default cooldown override. It doesn't send messages or convert anything."},{"id":"ld18","text":"Three leads are all engineers at the same prospect company. How do you keep them together?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Merge them into one lead"},{"id":"b","text":"Link each lead to the same Account — select them in the table and use Assign to account","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Put the other two names in the first lead's notes"},{"id":"d","text":"Convert them all to customers"}],"explanation":"Accounts group several leads under one parent company. Tick the three rows in the leads table, click Assign to account, and pick (or create) the account. You can also set the account on a single lead when creating or editing it."},{"id":"ld19","text":"On the Samples page, a sample has been Delivered and the prospect asked for a formal quote. What do you do?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Click the advance arrow again"},{"id":"b","text":"Open the row's three-dot outcome menu and choose Mark Quoted from sample","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Delete the sample now that it's done"},{"id":"d","text":"Nothing — it updates itself when the quote is sent"}],"explanation":"The advance arrow only moves a sample through Requested → Approved → Shipped → Delivered. Outcomes are deliberate, so they live in the three-dot menu: Mark Quoted from sample, Mark Lost from sample, or Mark Stale. A delivered sample with no outcome after 14 days is marked Stale for you."},{"id":"ld20","text":"A prospect on the Suppression list has an email opt-out but says they're happy to take phone calls. What should you do?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Nothing — the lead is on the list, so no contact of any kind is allowed"},{"id":"b","text":"Nothing needs to change — opt-outs are per channel, so calling is already allowed; lift the email opt-out only if they ask for email again","isCorrect":true},{"id":"c","text":"Delete the lead and create a new one"},{"id":"d","text":"Clear the cooldown to allow calls"}],"explanation":"Suppression is per channel. An email opt-out only blocks email; calls and texts are unaffected. Each row shows a red label per suppressed channel and a lift button for each one. A cooldown is a separate wait window and isn't what blocks a channel."}]}"""
        });

        // ── New Lead Fork (Article) ──────────────────────────────────────
        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Adding a Lead: Engagement Shapes",
            Slug = "leads-new-lead-fork",
            Summary = "How the New Lead dialog works: pick an engagement shape (Quick add, Quick quote/RFQ, Repeat, Strategic, Prototype) and fill the shape-specific fields tailored to that sales motion.",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Article,
            EstimatedMinutes = 5,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 5,
            AppRoutes = """["/leads"]""",
            Tags = """["leads","sales","intake"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Adding a Lead: Engagement Shapes\n\nWhen you click **New Lead** on the Leads page, you don't land on a flat form. A two-step panel opens that first asks *what kind of opportunity this is*, then shows a base form plus a few extra fields tailored to that sales motion. Picking the right shape up front means you capture the context that actually matters for that kind of deal — without burying every lead under fields most of them don't need.\n\n### Step 1 — Pick the Engagement Shape\n\nThe panel opens on the question \"What kind of opportunity is this?\" with five cards:\n\n- **Quick add** — Just the basics: company, contact, source. Skip the extras and tailor later. This is the fastest path. Pick this when you only have a name and want to get the lead into the system now.\n- **Quick quote / RFQ** — An inbound request with a formed need. Adds a parts/quantities list, a target due date, and a target price. Use this when a prospect already knows what they want quoted.\n- **Repeat / standing relationship** — An existing customer-shaped lead. Adds a reference-job field so you can point at the prior job number, PO, or work order this should reference.\n- **Strategic account** — A long sales cycle with multiple stakeholders. Adds decision-maker, champion, and current-vendor fields so the account context travels with the lead.\n- **Prototype / R&D** — Custom or exploratory work. Adds the RFQ fields *plus* project type and expected timeline.\n\nClicking a card advances you to Step 2.\n\n### Step 2 — Fill the Form\n\nEvery shape lands on the same base fields — the same set the rest of the Leads module uses:\n\n- **Lead Number** — shown only if your admin allows manual lead numbers; leave it blank and one is assigned for you.\n- **Company Name** and **Contact Name** — one of the two is required. For an individual with no company, the contact name alone is fine.\n- **Email**, **Phone** — the person you're working with. Email is checked for a valid format if you fill it in.\n- **Source** — how the lead found you, from the configurable lead-source list.\n- **Follow-Up Date** — can't be in the past.\n- **Account** — optionally link this lead to a parent company (a B2B account). You can create a new account right here with the **New Account** button without leaving the panel.\n- **Notes** — free-form context.\n\nBelow the base fields, an **extras** section appears *only* when the shape you picked has shape-specific fields. Quick add shows no extras section at all — that's by design, it keeps the fast path fast.\n\n### Shape-Specific Extra Fields\n\n| Shape | Extra fields captured |\n|---|---|\n| Quick add | (none) |\n| Quick quote / RFQ | Parts / quantities, Target price, Needed-by date |\n| Repeat | Reference job (prior job #, PO, or work order) |\n| Strategic | Decision maker, Champion, Current vendor |\n| Prototype / R&D | Parts / quantities, Target price, Needed-by date, Project type, Expected timeline |\n\nThese extras are saved with the lead, and only the fields you actually fill are kept — empty ones stay blank.\n\n### What Carries Forward\n\nThe shape you pick is stored on the lead as its **engagement shape**. On the lead detail panel, an engagement-signal strip uses that shape to show a short playbook hint reminding you what to do next for that kind of deal. The shape is guidance, not a hard gate — you can still work any lead through the normal New → Contacted → Quoting pipeline regardless of how it started.\n\n### Required Fields\n\nOnly a **company name or contact name** is required, on every shape. The **Create lead** button stays off until that's filled; hover it to see what's still missing. Everything else — including all the shape-specific extras — is optional and can be filled in later from the lead panel.\n\n### Back and Cancel\n\n**Back** returns you to the shape picker (Step 1) so you can change your mind about the shape. **Cancel** closes the panel without creating anything. Your work is auto-saved as a draft as you type, so an accidental close doesn't lose it.","sections":[]}"""
        });

        // ── Bulk Intake (Walkthrough) ────────────────────────────────────
        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Leads Intake & Bulk Import",
            Slug = "leads-intake",
            Summary = "A guided tour of the bulk-intake page: pick a strategy, upload or paste a CSV, then preview for duplicates and import the valid rows.",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Walkthrough,
            EstimatedMinutes = 6,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 6,
            AppRoutes = """["/leads/intake"]""",
            Tags = """["leads","intake","import","walkthrough"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"appRoute":"/leads/intake","startButtonLabel":"Tour Bulk Intake","steps":[{"element":".intake__step:nth-of-type(1)","popover":{"title":"Step 1 — Pick How You Got Them","description":"Choose where this batch came from: cold call, cold email, trade show follow-up, webinar attendee, purchased list, or manual entry. Your choice sets which fields are required — cold calls need a phone, cold emails need an email. You can also add a campaign tag to group the batch for reporting.","side":"bottom"}},{"element":".intake__columns-help","popover":{"title":"Which Columns to Include","description":"Only the company name is required. Contact, email, phone, source, and notes are optional. Column headings don't have to match exactly — common names are accepted. Open this reference any time you're unsure of the format.","side":"top"}},{"element":".intake__upload-button","popover":{"title":"Upload a Spreadsheet File","description":"Click here to pick a CSV file from your computer. The first row must name the columns. Once the file is read, its rows appear below for review.","side":"bottom"}},{"element":".intake__upload + app-textarea","popover":{"title":"Or Paste Rows","description":"Instead of a file, paste rows straight in — handy for a quick list copied from a spreadsheet. The first line must name the columns.","side":"top"}},{"element":"[data-testid='intake-parse-paste']","popover":{"title":"Read the Pasted Rows","description":"After pasting, click to read the rows into the preview below. Any rows that couldn't be read are listed so you can fix them. A Reset button appears once rows are loaded, in case you want to start over.","side":"top"}},{"element":"app-empty-state","popover":{"title":"Step 3 — Preview, Then Import","description":"Once rows are loaded, a third step appears here. Click Run preview: each row is checked against your existing leads and customers, anyone who opted out, anyone still in a wait period, and duplicates within the batch, then labeled — Will create, Duplicate lead, Already a customer, Opted out, In cooldown, or Missing field. Nothing is added yet. When you're happy, click Import: only the 'Will create' rows are added, the rest are skipped for you, and you're taken back to the leads list.","side":"top"}}]}"""
        });

        // ── Call Queue (Article) ─────────────────────────────────────────
        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Working the Call Queue",
            Slug = "leads-call-queue",
            Summary = "The pull-based worker queue for high-volume outreach: pull a batch, dial leads, and disposition each with single-keystroke shortcuts.",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Article,
            EstimatedMinutes = 5,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 7,
            AppRoutes = """["/leads/queue"]""",
            Tags = """["leads","outreach","queue"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Working the Call Queue\n\nThe Worker Queue (Leads → Worker Queue) is built for high-volume outreach — when you're working through dozens of leads a session and need to move fast. Instead of scrolling a list and opening each lead, you **pull a batch** of leads and the queue serves them to you one at a time, with keyboard shortcuts that record the outcome and advance automatically.\n\n### Pulling a Batch\n\nWhen the queue is empty it shows a \"Ready to work\" card. Set the **Pull size** (1–50, default 5) and click **Pull next N**. You're handed that many leads to work. If a teammate is dialing the same list at the same time, the queue hands each of you a different set of leads — you'll never both land on the same one, so nobody double-dials a prospect.\n\nIf there's nothing to pull, the queue tells you so (\"No more leads to pull right now\") — check back later or work a different campaign.\n\n### The Lead Card\n\nEach lead is shown as a single focused card with a progress indicator (\"Lead 2 of 5\") at the top. The card surfaces what you need to make the call:\n\n- **Company name** and any **campaign** label.\n- **Warning labels** — a cooldown-until date, email opt-out, or call opt-out. These tell you immediately if a lead shouldn't be contacted a certain way.\n- **Contact, email, source, last activity, and notes** in a compact grid.\n- A **call** button on the phone number. Clicking it opens the number in your computer's phone or softphone app.\n- A **Disposition notes** box for anything worth recording about the call.\n\n### Dispositioning a Lead\n\nAfter the call, record the outcome. Six dispositions are available — each as a button *and* a single-keystroke shortcut:\n\n| Key | Disposition | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| **E** | Engaged | The lead exits the queue and moves from New to Contacted — you made a real connection. |\n| **N** | No answer | Puts the lead back in the queue for a later try. |\n| **V** | Voicemail left | Puts the lead back in the queue for a later try. |\n| **C** | Schedule callback | Opens a panel to pick a date and time; it becomes the lead's follow-up date once you confirm. |\n| **B** | Bad data | Marks the lead Lost with the reason \"Bad data\" — wrong number, bounce, or disconnected line. |\n| **S** | Suppress (DNC) | The prospect asked not to be contacted. The request is recorded on the lead and it leaves the queue. |\n\nEvery disposition is one action, and the queue **automatically advances** to the next lead in your batch. The disposition notes you typed are attached to the lead's activity history, then cleared for the next lead.\n\n### Keyboard-First Navigation\n\nThe queue is designed to be worked without the mouse:\n\n- **E / N / V / C / B / S** fire the matching disposition.\n- **J** moves to the next lead in the batch; **K** moves to the previous one.\n- Shortcuts are ignored while you're typing in the notes box, so your typing is never intercepted.\n\nThe little keyboard badges on each button aren't decoration — they're the live shortcut keys.\n\n### When the Batch Is Exhausted\n\nWork through all the leads in your batch and the queue returns to the \"Ready to work\" card so you can pull the next batch. Nothing is lost between batches — each pull is a fresh, separate set of leads.\n\n### Where Dispositions Go\n\nEngaged leads become Contacted in the normal pipeline. No-answer and voicemail leads come back around for retry. Callback-scheduled leads carry their callback time as the follow-up date. Bad-data leads are Lost. Suppressed leads leave the queue; check the Suppression page to confirm the prospect's channel opt-outs are in place.","sections":[]}"""
        });

        // ── Outreach Campaigns (Article) ─────────────────────────────────
        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Outreach Campaigns",
            Slug = "leads-campaigns",
            Summary = "What outreach campaigns are, how they group bulk-import cohorts for reporting, and how to create and edit them.",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Article,
            EstimatedMinutes = 4,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 8,
            AppRoutes = """["/leads/campaigns"]""",
            Tags = """["leads","outreach","campaigns","marketing"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Outreach Campaigns\n\nA **Campaign** wraps a batch of marketing leads into a named cohort so you can configure outreach once and report on results cleanly. When you bulk-import leads, every lead is tagged with its campaign so reporting can answer questions like \"How did the September LinkedIn batch perform versus August?\" without guessing.\n\nFind campaigns at **Leads → Campaigns**.\n\n### What a Campaign Carries\n\nA campaign holds the configuration shared by every lead in the cohort:\n\n- **Name** (required) — a human label for the cohort (e.g., \"IMTS 2026 Booth Scans\").\n- **Description** — free-form context about the campaign.\n- **Strategy** — the outreach motion (Cold Call, Cold Email, Trade Show Follow-up, Webinar Attendee, Purchased List, Manual Entry). The strategy is set at creation and isn't changed afterward, since it defines how leads in the cohort were sourced.\n- **Default cooldown override** — how many days (0–730) to wait before re-contacting a lead in this campaign, overriding the system default.\n- **Started / Ended dates** — the active window of the campaign.\n- **Active** flag — whether the campaign is currently running.\n\n### The Campaigns List\n\nThe list is a sortable table with columns for Name, Strategy, Leads (the count of leads in the cohort), Active status, Started date, and Created date. The Active column shows a green \"Active\" or a gray \"Inactive\" label at a glance.\n\n### Creating a Campaign\n\nClick **New Campaign** in the page header. Fill in the name (required), an optional description, pick the strategy, optionally set a default cooldown override, and set the started/ended dates. Save and the campaign appears in the list.\n\nThe **Strategy** field only appears when creating — once leads are tied to a campaign under a given strategy, you don't switch it.\n\n### Editing a Campaign\n\nClick any row (or its edit icon) to open it for editing. You can update the name, description, cooldown override, and dates, and switch the **Active** flag on or off to pause or resume the campaign. The strategy can't be changed once set.\n\n### How Campaigns Connect to Intake and Leads\n\nCampaigns are the reporting backbone for bulk outreach:\n\n- On the **Bulk Intake** page you can tag an import batch with a campaign so the imported leads roll up to it.\n- On a **lead's panel**, a campaign label shows which campaign produced the lead and links straight to the Campaigns page. That label is read-only on the lead — the campaign is the source of record, set at intake, not reassigned from the lead.\n- In the **Worker Queue**, each lead card shows its campaign label so you know which cohort you're dialing.\n- The **default cooldown override** flows through to the suppression/cooldown logic for leads in the campaign, so re-contact timing respects the campaign's policy.","sections":[]}"""
        });

        // ── Accounts & Bulk-Assign (Article) ─────────────────────────────
        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Accounts & Bulk-Assigning Leads",
            Slug = "leads-accounts",
            Summary = "B2B Accounts group multiple leads under one parent company; how to create accounts, link leads to them, and bulk-assign many leads at once.",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Article,
            EstimatedMinutes = 4,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 9,
            AppRoutes = """["/leads/accounts","/leads"]""",
            Tags = """["leads","accounts","b2b"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Accounts & Bulk-Assigning Leads\n\n**Accounts** are parent-company groupings for B2B selling. When one prospect company has several people you're working — an engineer, a procurement contact, an executive sponsor — each of them is a lead, and an Account lets all of those leads roll up under one company umbrella.\n\nFind accounts at **Leads → Accounts**.\n\n### What an Account Holds\n\nAn account describes the parent company:\n\n- **Account name** (required), **Industry**, **Size bracket**.\n- **Address** — street, city, state, postal code, country.\n- **Website**, **Phone**, **Description**.\n\nThe accounts list table shows Name, Industry, Size, City, State, a **Contacts** count, a **Leads** count (how many leads point at this account), and the created date. The Contacts count reflects contact records stored on the account by the system; there's no screen for editing those contacts here today.\n\n### Creating an Account\n\nClick **New Account** in the toolbar, fill in the name (required) and any company details, and save. You can also create an account right from the New Lead panel's account picker — click **New Account** there to add one without leaving the lead you're creating.\n\n### Editing an Account\n\nClick any row to open it for editing. Change the details and save.\n\n### Linking a Lead to an Account\n\nThere are three ways a lead gets tied to an account:\n\n1. **When you create it** — the New Lead panel has an Account picker. Pick an existing account, or create one on the spot.\n2. **When you edit it** — the Edit Lead panel has the same picker.\n3. **In bulk** — from the main Leads list, select multiple leads and assign them to an account in one action (below).\n\nWhen a linked lead is later converted to a Customer, any contacts stored on the account roll forward as non-primary contacts on the new Customer, so the relationship survives conversion.\n\n### Bulk-Assigning Leads to an Account\n\nOn the main **Leads** list (table view), each row has a checkbox. Select one or more leads and a **bulk action bar** appears above the table showing how many are selected. Click **Assign to account**, pick the target account (you can create a new one here too), and click **Assign**. Every selected lead is linked to that account in one step. Pick **— None —** instead to unlink the selected leads.\n\nThis is the fast way to organize a pile of freshly imported leads — select the ones that belong to the same parent company and assign them together.\n\n### Deleting an Account\n\nUse the trash icon on the row. You **can't delete** an account while any lead is still linked to it — you'll get a message telling you how many leads are linked. Unassign the account on those leads first, then try again. Already-converted leads aren't affected by deleting an account.","sections":[]}"""
        });

        // ── Sample Shipment Tracking (Article) ───────────────────────────
        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Sample Shipment Tracking",
            Slug = "leads-samples",
            Summary = "The cross-lead view of pre-quote sample parts: the sample lifecycle, status filtering, advancing samples, and recording outcomes.",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Article,
            EstimatedMinutes = 4,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 10,
            AppRoutes = """["/leads/samples"]""",
            Tags = """["leads","samples","presales"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Sample Shipment Tracking\n\nBefore a quote is firm, you often send a prospect a **sample part** to evaluate. The Sample Shipments page (Leads → Samples) is the cross-lead view of every sample in flight — so sales managers can spot stale samples (delivered but no follow-up quote) and reps can see where each prospect sits in the sample-evaluation loop.\n\n### What the List Shows\n\nThe table lists every sample across all leads, with columns for the **Lead** it belongs to, **Part** description, **Qty**, **Status**, the **Requested / Shipped / Delivered** dates, **Cost** to us, and the **Tracking** number (carrier + tracking). Clicking a row opens that sample's lead.\n\n### The Sample Lifecycle\n\nA sample moves through a linear lifecycle, then ends in one of three outcomes:\n\n1. **Requested** — a sample has been asked for.\n2. **Approved** — the request is approved to send.\n3. **Shipped** — the sample is on its way (a ship date is stamped).\n4. **Delivered** — the prospect has it (a delivery date is stamped).\n\nThen an outcome:\n\n- **Quoted** (Quoted from sample) — the sample led to a quote. Success.\n- **Lost** (Lost from sample) — the sample didn't convert.\n- **Stale** — delivered but went quiet with no follow-up.\n\n### Advancing a Sample\n\nThe linear steps move forward one at a time. On each row, an **advance arrow** moves the sample to its next status — Requested → Approved → Shipped → Delivered. Hover the arrow to see which status is next. Advancing to Shipped or Delivered stamps the matching date automatically. Once a sample reaches Delivered (or an outcome), the advance arrow disappears.\n\n### Recording an Outcome\n\nRecording an outcome is a deliberate call you make, so it lives behind the row's **outcome menu** (the three-dot button) rather than the advance arrow:\n\n- **Mark Quoted from sample** — the sample produced a quote.\n- **Mark Lost from sample** — it didn't convert.\n- **Mark Stale** — manually flag it as gone cold.\n\nNote that **Stale** is also set for you automatically once a delivered sample sits for 14 days with no outcome, so you usually don't have to mark it by hand.\n\n### Filtering\n\nThe status filter at the top narrows the list. Beyond filtering to a single status, two convenience options help:\n\n- **All** — every sample.\n- **Open (not yet outcome)** — everything still in the active lifecycle (Requested through Delivered), hiding the Quoted / Lost / Stale outcomes. This is the view managers use to see what's genuinely in flight.\n\n### Where Samples Come From\n\nThis page is for tracking and moving samples through their lifecycle, not creating them. There is no button on this page or on the lead panel to request a new sample today — sample records are added through Forge's integration interface, so if your shop needs to log a new sample, ask your admin how it's set up for you.","sections":[]}"""
        });

        // ── Suppression / Do-Not-Contact (Article) ───────────────────────
        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Suppression & Do-Not-Contact",
            Slug = "leads-suppression",
            Summary = "The per-channel opt-out and cooldown list: what the channels mean, how the list is used, and how to lift a suppression or clear a cooldown.",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Article,
            EstimatedMinutes = 4,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 11,
            AppRoutes = """["/leads/suppression"]""",
            Tags = """["leads","suppression","compliance","dnc"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Suppression & Do-Not-Contact\n\nThe Suppression / Do Not Contact page (Leads → Suppression) is where opt-outs and cooldown windows live. It exists so you don't re-contact someone who asked you not to, and so re-contact timing respects the cooldown rules. New opt-outs and cooldowns accumulate here as they're recorded on leads.\n\n### Per-Channel Suppression\n\nSuppression is **per channel**, not all-or-nothing. A lead can opt out of one channel while staying reachable on another:\n\n- **Email opt-out** — don't email this lead.\n- **Call opt-out** — don't call this lead.\n- **SMS opt-out** — don't text this lead.\n\nThe Suppression column on each row shows a red label for every channel the lead has opted out of. A lead might show an email opt-out but still be callable, for example.\n\n### Cooldowns\n\nSeparate from a hard opt-out is a **cooldown** — a temporary \"don't contact until\" window. A lead in cooldown shows a yellow label with the cooldown-until date, and the Reason column explains why. Cooldowns come from outreach policy (for instance, a campaign's default cooldown override) and the queue and intake logic respect them: a lead still in cooldown is held back from the call queue and skipped during bulk import.\n\n### How the List Is Used\n\nThe opt-out flags and cooldown dates are stored as each lead's outreach preferences. Wherever outreach happens, they're checked:\n\n- **Bulk Intake** labels a row *Opted out* or *In cooldown* and skips it.\n- The **Worker Queue** shows the opt-out and cooldown labels on the lead card so you see them before you dial.\n- When a lead is **converted**, its preferences carry over to the new customer contact so the opt-out isn't lost.\n\nIn the call queue, the **Suppress** disposition records that the prospect asked not to be contacted and takes the lead out of your batch. After that, check this page to confirm the right channel opt-outs are showing for that lead.\n\n### Reviewing the List\n\nThe table shows the company, contact, email, phone, the suppression labels, the cooldown-until date, the cooldown reason, and when the preferences were last updated. Clicking a row opens that lead so you can see the full picture.\n\n### Lifting a Suppression\n\nWhen an opt-out was a mistake or the prospect has re-engaged, you can re-enable outreach **one channel at a time** from the row's action buttons:\n\n- **Lift email opt-out** (mail icon) — re-enables email.\n- **Lift call opt-out** (phone icon) — re-enables calling.\n- **Lift SMS opt-out** (sms icon) — re-enables texting.\n\nEach button only appears when that channel is actually suppressed. Lifting one channel leaves the others untouched. The change is recorded in the lead's activity log so there's an audit trail of who re-enabled what.\n\n### Clearing a Cooldown\n\nIf a lead is in a cooldown window you want to end early, the **Clear cooldown** button (clock icon) removes the cooldown-until date so the lead becomes contactable again immediately. This is independent of the channel opt-outs — clearing a cooldown doesn't touch any opt-out flags.","sections":[]}"""
