            Tags = """["leads","qualification","classification","itar","nda"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Lead Qualification & Classification\n\nOpen any lead's panel and below the basic info you'll find two qualification strips: a **classification strip** for tagging the lead's manufacturing and compliance fit, and an **engagement-signal strip** that tells you, at a glance, what to do next. Together they turn a raw contact into a qualified, prioritized opportunity.\n\n### Classification Labels\n\nThe classification strip carries up to four colored labels. The campaign label is read-only; the other three are click-to-set — click the label, pick a value from the menu, and it saves right away.\n\n**Capability fit** — can your shop actually make what this prospect needs?\n- *Not assessed* — the starting value, nobody has judged it yet.\n- *Fits* — squarely in your wheelhouse.\n- *Needs review* — an engineer should look before you commit.\n- *Doesn't fit* — outside your capabilities; qualify out early rather than waste a quote.\n\n**NDA** — where the non-disclosure agreement stands:\n- *None*, *Requested*, *In force*, *Expired*. Track this so you don't share sensitive specs before an NDA is in force, and so you notice when one lapses.\n\n**ITAR / export control** — export-control clearance for defense or controlled work:\n- *N/A*, *Pending*, *Cleared*, *Denied*. For ITAR-controlled work this matters before any technical data changes hands.\n\n**Campaign** (read-only) — if the lead came from a bulk-marketing campaign, this label names it and links to the Campaigns page. It's set at intake and isn't reassigned from the lead.\n\nEach label is color-coded, so a row of green labels reads very differently from a row with a red \"Doesn't fit\" or \"Denied\". Setting a label is one click and is recorded in the lead's activity history.\n\n### The Engagement-Signal Strip\n\nNear the top of the detail body, an engagement strip gives working guidance. It's shown only while the lead is active (hidden once the lead is Converted or Lost, since the guidance no longer applies):\n\n- **Playbook hint** — driven by the lead's engagement shape (Quick quote, Strategic, Prototype, etc.). It reminds you what this kind of deal needs next — e.g., a strategic account's hint nudges you toward mapping the decision-maker and champion.\n- **Recent-engagement label** — when the system has counted recent communications on the lead, a label shows the count so you know whether the lead has been actively worked.\n- **Stale badge** — appears when the system flags the lead as having gone quiet. It's a prompt to follow up or reclassify before the opportunity goes cold.\n\nThe playbook hint always shows for a shaped lead. The two labels depend on communication tracking being in place for your install, so don't be surprised if they're absent — the lead's Recent communications and Activity history sections below are the reliable place to see what's happened.\n\n### Why This Matters\n\nThe classification labels and engagement signals are how a lead earns its place in your work. A lead that fits your capabilities, has its NDA in force, is export-clearable, and shows recent engagement is a strong opportunity worth your time. One that doesn't fit, or has gone quiet for weeks, is a candidate to qualify out or re-prioritize. Keeping these tags current means the team spends effort where it pays off — and the compliance flags (NDA, ITAR) keep you from sharing controlled information before you're cleared to.","sections":[]}"""
