            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Reference,
            EstimatedMinutes = 4,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 90,
            AppRoutes = """["/leads"]""",
            Tags = """["leads","reference","advanced"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Leads — Advanced Reference\n\nThe deeper how-to for reps who work leads all day. For everyday basics see the Overview, Walkthrough, and Field Reference modules; this page covers the tips, gotchas, and edge cases you hit once you're moving real volume.\n\n## Getting a Messy Spreadsheet to Import Cleanly\n\nWhen you bulk-import leads, you don't have to reformat your spreadsheet first. The import reads your column headings loosely — capitalization doesn't matter, and common wordings all work. \"Company\", \"Company Name\", and \"companyname\" are all read as the company. \"Email\" and \"email address\" both work; so do \"Phone\", \"phone number\", and \"tel\". The only column that has to be there is the company name; contact, email, phone, source, and notes are all optional. Any heading it doesn't recognize is simply left out — so an extra column you don't need won't break the import.\n\nBefore anything is added, the import previews every row and labels it so you can see at a glance what will happen. The labels tell you why a row will or won't be brought in:\n\n- **Will create** — the row is good and gets added when you import.\n- **Missing field** — the field your outreach type needs is blank. A cold-call batch needs a phone; a cold-email batch needs an email.\n- **Invalid** — the row can't be used as-is, for example no company name at all.\n- **Duplicate in batch** — the same email or phone shows up earlier in the same file, so you're not importing it twice.\n- **Duplicate lead** — the email or phone already matches a lead you have. (Email matching ignores upper/lower case; phone matches on the digits, so formatting differences don't fool it.)\n- **Already a customer** — the email or phone belongs to a contact on a customer you already sell to.\n- **Opted out** — this person asked not to be contacted on the channel your batch uses.\n- **In cooldown** — this person is still inside a don't-contact-until window.\n\nImporting brings in only the \"Will create\" rows and skips the rest for you. Each new lead gets a note on its activity history showing it came in through a bulk import, so later you know how it arrived.\n\n## Call-Queue Keyboard Cheat Sheet\n\nThe call queue (Leads → Worker Queue) is built to work without the mouse. Letter keys record an outcome; J and K move between leads. Shortcuts are ignored while you're typing in the notes box, so your typing is never caught.\n\n| Key | Outcome | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| E | Engaged | Leaves the queue and moves a New lead to Contacted. |\n| N | No answer | Goes back in the queue for a later try. |\n| V | Voicemail left | Goes back in the queue for a later try. |\n| C | Schedule callback | Opens the callback panel; the date and time become the follow-up date once you confirm. |\n| B | Bad data | Marks the lead Lost with the reason \"Bad data\" (wrong number or bounce). |\n| S | Suppress (DNC) | Records the do-not-contact request on the lead and takes it out of the queue. |\n| J | (move) | Go to the next lead in your batch. |\n| K | (move) | Go back to the previous lead. |\n\nEach outcome is one step and moves you on automatically. Batches don't overlap between reps, so two reps never land on the same lead.\n\n## How a Suppression or Wait Period Lifts\n\nEach channel — email, call, and text — is handled on its own, so you can re-enable one without touching the others:\n\n- Each opt-out records when it happened and where it came from; lifting it clears both. Each change is noted in the lead's activity history.\n- A **cooldown** is a separate wait window that applies no matter the channel. Clearing the cooldown-until date is noted too, along with its reason.\n\nThe full loop plays out like this: an opt-out or cooldown is recorded on the lead's outreach preferences (a campaign policy can set the cooldown). That holds the lead back from the call queue and from bulk import while it's active — so nobody re-dials someone who asked you to stop. Later, from the Suppression page, you lift it one channel at a time or clear the cooldown. Each lift or clear is its own step, saved on that lead and written to its activity history, so there's a clear record of who re-enabled what and when.\n\n## When a Sample Goes Stale on Its Own\n\nYou don't have to police every sample yourself. A sample moves from **Delivered** to **Stale** for you once it was delivered more than 14 days ago and still has no outcome — no quote written from it and no loss recorded. The change is written to the sample's lead activity so you can see it went cold. The 14-day window is fixed; it gives the prospect time to evaluate without letting a sample quietly slip. You can still mark a sample stale by hand from its outcome menu — the sweep just catches the ones you didn't get back to.\n\n## Why an Account Won't Delete\n\nYou can't delete an account while any lead still points at it — you'll get a message saying how many leads are linked and asking you to unassign the account on each one first. This keeps the link intact for history. Unassign the account on those leads (edit the lead, or select them in the table and assign '— None —'), then try again. Converted leads no longer count.\n\n## How Engagement-Shape Fields Carry\n\nA lead remembers which New-Lead shape created it — Quick add, Quick quote, Repeat, Strategic, or Prototype (Quick add is the default). The shape-specific extras you filled in (parts and quantities, target price, needed-by date, decision-maker, champion, current vendor, project type, timeline) stay with the lead; fields you left blank simply aren't kept. When you create the lead, its shape is noted in the activity history, and the lead panel uses that shape to show you the playbook hint for that kind of deal.","sections":[]}"""
